  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Determine which right-angled triangles have side lengths that are all consecutive even integers. Hint: let $n$ be the side length of the shortest side.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p9-q5-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Only the triangle with side lengths 6, 8 and 10.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p9-q5-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Apply Pythagoras to consecutive even lengths and retain the positive solution.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "The side lengths are $n$, $n+2$ and $n+4$, with $n+4$ the hypotenuse.\n$$\\begin{align*}n^2+(n+2)^2&=(n+4)^2\\\\n^2+n^2+4n+4&=n^2+8n+16\\\\n^2-4n-12&=0\\\\(n-6)(n+2)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $n=6$ or $n=-2$. Since a side length is positive, $n=6$.\nThe only possible side lengths are $6$, $8$ and $10$. Check: $6^2+8^2=100=10^2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
